xample of Osthoff's law, which predicts shortening of long vowels when followed by a resonant and then a consonant. Thus, the thematicized pre-form *h₂weh₁n̥tos could have yielded *wēntos, which would have eventually shortened into *wentos. However, the existence of Osthoff's law in Latin is disputed, and one alternative proposal suggests that *h₂weh₁n̥tos would have instead directly yielded *wentos. According to th…","tpl":[{"k":52,"lang":56,"form":"*wē̆ntos"},{"k":52,"lang":48,"form":"*h₂wéh₁n̥ts","gloss":"blowing"},{"k":42,"lang":"en","form":"wind"},{"k":42,"lang":"sa","form":"वात॑"},{"k":42,"lang":"ae","form":"𐬬𐬁𐬙𐬀"},{"k":42,"lang":45,"form":"ᾰ̓είς"},{"k":42,"lang":"la","form":"vannus"}]},"rel":{"d":["ventōsus","ventulus"]}},{"w":"ventus","p":"NOUN","e":2,"s":[{"g":"arrival","t":250,"x":["Venilia à veniendo, ac ventu illo, quem Plautus dicit: Quod ille dixit, qui secundo vento vectus est tranquillo mari, ventum gaudeo."]}],"f":[["ventūs",2],["ventus",7,3],["ventūs",13,3],["ventūs",3,3],["ventuum",12,3],["ventuī",6,3],["ventibus",11,3],["ventum",5,3],["ventūs",10,3],["ventū",4,3],["ventibus",9,3],["ventus",8,3],["ventūs",14,3]],"tpl":["la-ndecl"],"ipa":[{"i":"[ˈwɛn.tʊs]","t":0},{"i":"[ˈvɛn.tus]","t":1}],"ety":{"text":"Inherited from Proto-Italic *gʷemtus, from Proto-Indo-European *gʷém-tu-s, from *gʷem- (“to come”). The -n- was, according to De Vaan, potentially adopted from the verb veniō (“to come”). By surface analysis, veniō + -tus (forming action nouns).","tpl":[{"k":52,"lang":56,"form":"*gʷemtus"},{"k":52,"lang":48,"form":"*gʷémtus"}]}}],"virga":[{"w":"virga","p":"NOUN","s":[{"g":"twig, young shoot","t":63},{"g":"rod, switch for flogging.","t":63,"x":["flagellum equō et cāmus asinō et virgā dorsō inprūdentium"]},{"g":"staff, walking stick","t":63,"x":["8 AD . Fasti, Publius Ovidius Naso, Liber II, 703-704. Illic Tarquinius mandata latentia nati Accipit, et virga lilia summa metit. There Tarquinius is receiving secret mandates of the son And is knocking down with staff the tops of lilies."]},{"g":"wand (magical)","t":63,"x":["Tum virgam capit: Hāc animās ille ēvocat Orcō pallentīs, aliās sub Tartara trīstia mittit, [...]."]},{"g":"penis","t":228,"x":["sunt etiam singularia in medio constituta ne in unam partem praeiudicialiter vergentia alteram competenti decore nudarent: nasus, os, guttur, pectus, umbilicus, et genitalium virga descendens, quae laudabilia et honora monstrantur quando in medio locata consistunt."]},{"g":"a unit of measurement, a rod; of either 3 feet in length, or 16½ feet, also known as a pole or perch; a square of land of the same length and breadth","t":231}],"f":[["virgae",2],["virga",7,3],["virgae",13,3],["virgae",3,3],["virgārum",12,3],["virgae",6,3],["virgīs",11,3],["virgam",5,3],["virgās",10,3],["virgā",4,3],["virgīs",9,3],["virga",8,3],["virgae",14,3]],"tpl":["la-ndecl"],"ipa":[{"i":"[ˈwɪr.ɡa]","t":0},{"i":"[ˈvir.ɡa]","t":1}],"ety":{"text":"Uncertain. Traditionally, the term is derived from earlier *wizgā, itself from Proto-Indo-European *wis-g-, an extension of *weys-. De Vaan suggests a possible connection with Germanic forms such as Old Norse visk or Old English wisc, perhaps also entailing a PIE form of the shape *wis-g-. The term might be related to Latin vireō and virāgō.","tpl":[{"k":81,"lang":"la"},{"k":42,"lang":124,"form":"visk"},{"k":42,"lang":96,"form":"wisc"},{"k":42,"lang":"la","form":"vireō"}]},"rel":{"d":["virgāta","virgātor","virgātus","virgeus","virgō","virgōsus","virgula"]}}]}}
